'''Final Boss Giga-God''' (ファイナルギガグランド神さま, ''Fainaru Giga Gurando Kami-sama'', '''Final Giga Grand God''') is a [[:Category:Traitless Enemies|Traitless]] enemy seen as the boss of [[The Big Bang (Cats of the Cosmos)|The Big Bang]] in [[Cats of the Cosmos]] Chapter 3. He was added in [[Version 8.3 Update|Version 8.3]].

{{Enemy Info
|name = Final Boss Giga-God
|image = E 446.png
|appearance = '''CotC''': [[The Big Bang (Cats of the Cosmos)|Chapter 3 - The Big Bang]]
|money drop = 6,000¢ - 23,700¢
}}

==Enemy==
Final Boss Giga-God has less health than his two previous iterations, [[The Cat God]] and [[Cat God]], while maintaining the five knockbacks. He stands at a massive range and has an attack pattern similar to the second Cat God, performing two weak attacks (the first of which carries a 1,500-range Warp and generates a level 12 [[Wave Attack]]) before following it up with the final strike that contains most of his damage, so he must be fought reactively.

Like his other forms, Giga-God has a buff of 1,100% without the [[Mystery Mask]], causing him to have '''10,999,989 HP''' and deal a combined total of '''813,967 damage'''.

==Strategy==
The [[Mesocosmocyclone]]s will teleport your cats forward to let Giga-God generate his Warping Wave Attack and let the peons push to your base, but you can send weak meatshields or [[:Category:Cats with Warp Immunity|Warp-immune cats]] to be knocked back instead of being teleported forward by Mesocosmocyclones to avoid generating the wave. [[Rover Cat (Rare Cat)|Catellite]] is not the best at doing this due to its singular knockback, but can instead stall the Mesocosmocyclones.

Another way to deal with his wave is staying near your base. While he teleport some cats, fast meatshields like [[Crazed Cat (Super Rare Cat)|Crazed Macho Cat]] or [[Crazed Tank Cat (Super Rare Cat)|Crazed Wall Cat]] can reach Giga-God before he finishes his animation shortly after they leave the base, stalling him pretty successfully.

Rush units with minimal foreswing like [[Express Cat (Super Rare Cat)|Maglev Cat]] and [[Bahamut Cat (Special Cat)|Awakened Bahamut Cat]] are pretty effective here, because the warp will usually reset their attack animation, allowing them to do a little more damage. Under ideal circumstances, the rushers might be able to inflict enough damage to interrupt Giga-God, causing even more mayhem as he repeats his first hit.

